## DESCRIPTION
## Calculus Two, Sequences
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Infinite sequences and series)
## DBsection(Limits of sequences)
## Date(09/14/2018)
## Institution(Community College of Denver, Colorado Community College System)
## Author(Brittni Lorton)
## KEYWORDS('calculus', 'sequences', 'formula of a sequence')

###########################
#  Initialization

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros("PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "PGcourse.pl");

Context('Numeric')->variables->are(n => "Real");

$a = random(1, 3);
$b = non_zero_random(-2, 2);

if ($a == 1) {
    @aseq = (1, 0, -1, 0, 1, 0, -1, 0);
    $fa   = Formula("sin(pi*n/2)");
} elsif ($a == 2) {
    @aseq = (0, -1, 0, 1, 0, -1, 0, 1);
    $fa   = Formula("sin(pi/2(n+1))");
} else {
    @aseq = (-1, 0, 1, 0, -1, 0, 1, 0);
    $fa   = Formula("-sin(pi*n/2)");
}
$fa->{test_points} = [ [1], [2], [3], [4], [5], [6] ];
$fb                = Formula("$b*(-1)^(n-1)/(2*n-1)")->reduce;
$fb->{test_points} = [ [1], [2], [3], [4], [5], [6] ];

BEGIN_PGML

For the following sequences, [`\{a_n\}_{n=1}^{\infty}`], find a formula for the general term [`a_n`]. 

a) [`\{a_n\}=\{[@ join(', ', @aseq) @]\}`]
    
    [`a_n=`][_]{$fa}{15} 
    
    Hint: think of where [`\sin(x)`] or [`\cos(x)`] takes on those values.

b) [`\{b_n\}=\{[$b], -[$b]/3, [$b]/5, -[$b]/7,...\}`]

    [`b_n=`][_]{$fb}{15}

 [@ helpLink("formulas") @]*
END_PGML

if ($a == 1) {
    $explanation = "This is the sequence shown above.";
} elsif ($a == 2) {
    $explanation = "This is the sequence shifted right by 1.";
} else {
    $explanation = "This is the negative of the sequence, so we can multiply"
        . "\(f(n)\) by \(-1\).";
}

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
First note that both of the sequences start at [`n=1`]. 

a)  It's helpful to know that if  [`f(n) = \sin(n \pi/2)`] then

    [``` \{f(n)\}_{n=1}^{\infty} = \{1,0,-1,0,1,-1,0,1\}```]

    [$explanation]*

    [`` f(n) = [$fa] ``]

b) For this, note that the denominator has the form [`2n-1`]. 
The numerator is just [$b] changing sign, so the result is 

[``` g(n) = [$fb]```]


Note: these functions are not unique.  There are certain other correct answer forms. 
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
